    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ты сейчас серьёзно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you serious right now?' Kostya uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'seriously', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, я абсолютно серьёзна.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, I am completely serious.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'yes' with 'serious'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я этого не ожидал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I did not expect this.' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'expected'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тебе следовало этого ожидать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You should have expected it.' Notice how 'for you' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'to expect'. Then Kostya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Вчера всё было так хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yesterday everything was so good.' Kostya links 'yesterday' directly with 'everything' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вчера осталось в прошлом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yesterday is in the past.' Nastya combines 'yesterday' with 'last' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Что ты хочешь от меня?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What do you want from me?' The question opens with the interrogative word for 'what' and builds toward 'me'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хочу серьёзных отношений.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I want a serious relationship.' Nastya combines 'I' with 'relationships' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Kostya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я очень стараюсь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am trying my best.' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'try'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Этого недостаточно, Костя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It is not enough, Kostya.' Nastya links 'that' directly with 'not enough' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ты больше...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are not...' Kostya combines 'you' with 'more' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не что?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Not what?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'not' with 'what'. Then Kostya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
